1
00:00:24,152 --> 00:00:44,500
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is the Sutta in the Samyutta Nikaya, called the son's flesh, and this is about the four kinds of nutriment for the maintenance of being, that have already... from the maintenance of beings that have already come to be, and for the assistance of those about to come to be.

2
00:00:46,301 --> 00:00:50,763
[SPEAKER_00]: But I think it's for the maintenance of being, for the maintenance of your being.

3
00:00:53,848 --> 00:00:54,949
[SPEAKER_01]: But it made them into a bhava.

4
00:00:55,629 --> 00:00:55,869
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

5
00:00:57,470 --> 00:00:58,191
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what for?

6
00:00:58,871 --> 00:01:07,256
[SPEAKER_00]: The nutriment of food, of consuming, consumable food, edible food, gross or subtle.

7
00:01:08,817 --> 00:01:11,859
[SPEAKER_00]: Second is contact, phassa.

8
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:14,961
[SPEAKER_00]: Third is mental intention.

9
00:01:16,142 --> 00:01:17,563
[SPEAKER_00]: And fourth is consciousness.

10
00:01:23,447 --> 00:01:31,493
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in terms of the first nutriment of edible food, how should it be seen?

11
00:01:31,513 --> 00:01:33,955
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a simile that's given.

12
00:01:35,396 --> 00:01:41,541
[SPEAKER_00]: Suppose a couple, husband and wife, had taken limited provisions and were traveling through a desert.

13
00:01:42,622 --> 00:01:45,504
[SPEAKER_00]: They have with them their only son, dear and beloved.

14
00:01:46,104 --> 00:01:50,708
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, in the middle of the desert, their limited provisions would be used up and exhausted.

15
00:01:51,820 --> 00:01:55,241
[SPEAKER_00]: By eating our son's flesh, we can cross the rest of this desert.

16
00:02:17,427 --> 00:02:18,548
[SPEAKER_00]: There's some practical thing in there.

17
00:02:18,608 --> 00:02:28,896
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it says, then, because the husband and wife would kill their son, who they loved, prepare dried meat, and by eating their son's flesh, they would cross the rest of the desert.

18
00:02:30,077 --> 00:02:34,740
[SPEAKER_00]: But while they are eating their son's flesh, they would cry and become distraught.

19
00:02:35,461 --> 00:02:36,902
[SPEAKER_00]: Where are you, our only son?

20
00:02:37,362 --> 00:02:38,523
[SPEAKER_00]: Where are you, our only son?

21
00:02:38,603 --> 00:02:39,364
[SPEAKER_00]: What do you think?

22
00:02:39,444 --> 00:02:46,049
[SPEAKER_00]: Because would they eat that food for amusement, for enjoyment, for the sake of physical beauty and attractiveness?

23
00:02:46,750 --> 00:02:46,930
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

24
00:02:48,295 --> 00:02:50,896
[SPEAKER_00]: Would they eat the food for the sake of crossing the desert?

25
00:02:51,056 --> 00:02:51,256
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

26
00:02:52,736 --> 00:02:57,617
[SPEAKER_00]: It is in such a way, because I say that the nutriment of edible food should be seen.

27
00:02:58,137 --> 00:03:05,199
[SPEAKER_00]: When that nutriment of food is fully understood, lust for the five cords of sensual pleasure is fully understood.

28
00:03:05,959 --> 00:03:12,681
[SPEAKER_00]: When that's fully understood, there is no fetter bound by which a noble disciple can return, might return to this world.

29
00:03:16,766 --> 00:03:17,827
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so, there you go.

30
00:03:17,987 --> 00:03:19,968
[SPEAKER_01]: Complete dispassion towards food.

31
00:03:20,889 --> 00:03:21,149
[SPEAKER_01]: How?

32
00:03:21,329 --> 00:03:26,813
[SPEAKER_01]: By developing the context that will prevent you from having any passion towards it.

33
00:03:28,013 --> 00:03:28,874
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the simile.

34
00:03:28,894 --> 00:03:37,560
[SPEAKER_01]: The simile is not just a, you know, I don't know, some poetic, shocking emphasis on, like, how you should know.

35
00:03:37,600 --> 00:03:40,502
[SPEAKER_01]: It's, like, imagine eating your own child.

36
00:03:40,522 --> 00:03:42,263
[SPEAKER_01]: How would that feel for survival?

37
00:03:43,043 --> 00:03:45,645
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, now, take that context,

38
00:03:51,613 --> 00:03:53,434
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what you need to sustain.

39
00:03:53,514 --> 00:03:53,755
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

40
00:03:53,835 --> 00:04:12,668
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the mind will completely turn away from any enjoyment, amusement, any lust towards edible food, edible nutriment, which in return will completely remove any attachment and desire towards the five codes of sensual pleasure.

41
00:04:13,469 --> 00:04:14,790
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's what it all comes down to.

42
00:04:15,450 --> 00:04:15,811
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't...

43
00:04:15,831 --> 00:04:16,831
[SPEAKER_00]: Consumption of pleasure.

44
00:04:17,252 --> 00:04:17,672
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't be...

45
00:04:26,868 --> 00:04:27,348
[SPEAKER_01]: Survival.

46
00:04:27,368 --> 00:04:32,550
[SPEAKER_01]: You wouldn't eat for the sake of eating, for the sake of chewing, for the sake of consuming, for the sake of enjoying.

47
00:04:33,111 --> 00:04:35,011
[SPEAKER_01]: You would only have one goal in your mind.

48
00:04:41,054 --> 00:04:43,375
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

49
00:04:43,415 --> 00:04:51,018
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whenever you eat, whenever you're putting something in your mouth, that you are consuming, always bring that context up.

50
00:04:57,367 --> 00:04:57,867
[SPEAKER_01]: context up.

51
00:04:58,348 --> 00:05:02,450
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you'll see whether you need to eat, and how much, and whether...

52
00:05:02,730 --> 00:05:09,214
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, well, if there is any passion towards what you are eating, well, it won't be there, if you sustain this context.

53
00:05:09,254 --> 00:05:11,415
[SPEAKER_01]: Passion cannot survive in that environment.

54
00:05:14,677 --> 00:05:17,899
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not... yes, it's lovely food that I'm about to eat.

55
00:05:17,959 --> 00:05:18,459
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter.

56
00:05:18,699 --> 00:05:23,302
[SPEAKER_01]: Um, who knows, maybe even the boy taste is really nice, like, in terms of the actual taste.

57
00:05:23,842 --> 00:05:24,683
[SPEAKER_01]: But it doesn't mean that

58
00:05:29,055 --> 00:05:38,178
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not that the mouth won't perceive agreeability of the texture or food, but that's completely not the basis for passion.

59
00:05:39,578 --> 00:05:43,379
[SPEAKER_01]: Loss of context is the basis for passion, for the agreeability of the taste and smell.

60
00:05:43,399 --> 00:05:49,500
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'm eating, I'm taking, I'm putting this food in my mouth for this body to continue.

61
00:05:50,501 --> 00:05:50,721
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

62
00:05:52,141 --> 00:05:52,861
[SPEAKER_01]: So... Why?

63
00:05:54,382 --> 00:05:55,642
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do you need the body to continue?

64
00:05:58,293 --> 00:05:59,414
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, there's it.

65
00:05:59,434 --> 00:06:00,894
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I can practice the holy life.

66
00:06:01,274 --> 00:06:01,475
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

67
00:06:01,495 --> 00:06:03,495
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what does that practice of holy life entail?

68
00:06:03,656 --> 00:06:04,736
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you know what the practice is?

69
00:06:05,476 --> 00:06:07,377
[SPEAKER_01]: Or is it just the idea of...

70
00:06:07,457 --> 00:06:11,379
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm practicing by, you know, thinking about practice.

71
00:06:13,260 --> 00:06:15,021
[SPEAKER_01]: So, do you see what the practice is?

72
00:06:34,358 --> 00:06:36,819
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I would think that's the way I'm eating as well.

73
00:06:38,119 --> 00:06:39,959
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I wouldn't worry about the way you're eating.

74
00:06:40,960 --> 00:06:43,500
[SPEAKER_01]: If the context is there, doesn't matter which way you're eating.

75
00:06:44,241 --> 00:06:46,601
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, I'm not talking like slow, like slowly.

76
00:06:46,661 --> 00:06:47,601
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, what do you mean then?

77
00:06:47,781 --> 00:06:49,742
[SPEAKER_00]: Or... Yeah, that's what I thought.

78
00:06:49,802 --> 00:06:50,822
[SPEAKER_00]: Or chopsticks or something.

79
00:06:50,862 --> 00:06:51,282
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

80
00:06:51,302 --> 00:06:51,902
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you mean then?

81
00:06:52,623 --> 00:07:01,785
[SPEAKER_00]: No, so I'm having a context of this is going inside the body, for the body, not for... Oh, right, right, right.

82
00:07:02,101 --> 00:07:04,763
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, sure, sure.

83
00:07:05,303 --> 00:07:16,490
[SPEAKER_01]: But all of that is kind of psychologizing now, that, like, all of those insights, and the way you can describe it, are fine, if the context is there.

84
00:07:18,551 --> 00:07:31,499
[SPEAKER_01]: The context of eating for the right reason only, not too much, not too little, not overthinking it, certainly not giving in to the enjoyment, craving, and kind of emotional dependence on agreeable food.

85
00:07:33,058 --> 00:07:44,845
[SPEAKER_01]: But if that context is not there, then, yeah, I mean, you can analyze it, and you can visualize it, and you can contemplate how it's just in the body and stuff, but fundamentally, the passion is still there.

86
00:07:46,046 --> 00:07:48,627
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why the drastic simile that the Buddha gave.

87
00:07:49,688 --> 00:07:53,810
[SPEAKER_01]: Not about, you know, imagine the body's not yours, and you're providing food for it, and so on.

88
00:07:54,571 --> 00:07:55,211
[SPEAKER_01]: All that's true.

89
00:07:55,891 --> 00:08:00,474
[SPEAKER_01]: But it won't work, because the lust is, you know, rooted deeper than that.

90
00:08:01,501 --> 00:08:05,542
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what will work is when you don't want to eat it, but you have to.

91
00:08:07,583 --> 00:08:16,365
[SPEAKER_01]: And what will work even more when you don't want to eat it, but you have to, and what you have to eat is what was dearest to you, that's not meant to be eaten.

92
00:08:17,825 --> 00:08:23,346
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, it's like there's no... not a grain of your, you know, of your being would want to be eating it.

93
00:08:23,526 --> 00:08:25,687
[SPEAKER_01]: It will only eat it just to survive, purely.

94
00:08:28,332 --> 00:08:40,335
[SPEAKER_01]: Same, like, you know, the simile of the charcoal pit with burning embers, and two men come and want to drag you and throw you in, and then your mind will want to be anywhere else except there.

95
00:08:40,736 --> 00:08:44,617
[SPEAKER_01]: You would want... intention would go in any direction except to the pit.

96
00:08:44,877 --> 00:08:46,677
[SPEAKER_00]: So why can't I just enjoy my food?

97
00:08:47,538 --> 00:08:48,558
[SPEAKER_00]: Why can't I just eat it?

98
00:08:48,918 --> 00:08:49,818
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it doesn't belong to you.

99
00:08:51,719 --> 00:08:53,619
[SPEAKER_01]: My... the food?

100
00:08:54,079 --> 00:08:54,260
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

101
00:08:54,840 --> 00:08:55,860
[SPEAKER_01]: Your body doesn't belong to you.

102
00:08:57,084 --> 00:08:58,845
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how can you eat the food as if it's yours?

103
00:09:02,187 --> 00:09:03,528
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, you can eat it, if you want.

104
00:09:03,588 --> 00:09:04,769
[SPEAKER_01]: You can enjoy it, if you want.

105
00:09:05,850 --> 00:09:07,191
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like, why can't I?

106
00:09:07,331 --> 00:09:07,831
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, you can.

107
00:09:08,932 --> 00:09:10,473
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, you have to suffer the consequences.

108
00:09:12,154 --> 00:09:19,519
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, a fish suffers consequences when it eats the food that he, whatever, unwarily, that the fisherman puts on a bait.

109
00:09:21,500 --> 00:09:22,241
[SPEAKER_01]: Fish enjoys it.

110
00:09:22,481 --> 00:09:23,562
[SPEAKER_01]: As a result, it gets hooked.

111
00:09:24,537 --> 00:09:33,223
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not a... that we are outlining some moral law here, that you must hate your food and eat it like if it's your only child.

112
00:09:33,484 --> 00:09:34,164
[SPEAKER_01]: Do what you like.

113
00:09:34,524 --> 00:09:35,805
[SPEAKER_01]: Eat and enjoy if you want to.

114
00:09:37,407 --> 00:09:39,068
[SPEAKER_01]: But... What's the danger?

115
00:09:39,428 --> 00:09:40,049
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly.

116
00:09:40,969 --> 00:09:41,590
[SPEAKER_01]: There is danger.

117
00:09:41,610 --> 00:09:42,190
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the danger?

118
00:09:42,210 --> 00:09:43,591
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I'm not gonna tell you.

119
00:09:44,292 --> 00:09:45,032
[SPEAKER_01]: See it for yourself.

120
00:09:47,374 --> 00:09:49,155
[SPEAKER_01]: You already know that there is some danger.

121
00:09:49,295 --> 00:09:49,956
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the point.

122
00:09:52,721 --> 00:10:12,508
[SPEAKER_01]: Because why would otherwise, then, life, and food, and other causes of sensual pleasure, be so bent on pleasure, and ignoring pain, and outrunning pain, and sort of being completely... maintaining the certain level of unawareness of your own situation?

123
00:10:13,068 --> 00:10:18,710
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do people don't like to think about illness, their own illness, or even illness of people that are dear to them?

124
00:10:19,390 --> 00:10:21,511
[SPEAKER_01]: Mortality, aging... Why?

125
00:10:23,782 --> 00:10:25,543
[SPEAKER_01]: There's something there that people don't want to admit.

126
00:10:27,023 --> 00:10:30,564
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, chasing pleasure, or food, or otherwise, is tied to that.

127
00:10:31,445 --> 00:10:40,228
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you stop and think about it, you would see that maybe, you know, yeah, I could enjoy food, I could enjoy the senses, but what if there is a catch there?

128
00:10:41,868 --> 00:10:46,750
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, even the most untrained mind can see, like what we said in previous talks.

129
00:10:48,211 --> 00:10:52,012
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you don't have any valladhāma, no values, or precepts, or whatever else,

130
00:10:53,303 --> 00:10:59,305
[SPEAKER_01]: Everybody, at some point in their life, sooner or later, do get a recognition of what's the point.

131
00:11:00,466 --> 00:11:10,010
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, chasing the desires, again, food or otherwise, only results in a very, very temporary satisfaction.

132
00:11:10,690 --> 00:11:14,211
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's not even a satisfaction, it's more like forgetting about the pressure.

133
00:11:15,152 --> 00:11:16,132
[SPEAKER_01]: During the period.

134
00:11:16,572 --> 00:11:18,353
[SPEAKER_01]: During or a little bit out, doesn't matter.

135
00:11:18,433 --> 00:11:21,434
[SPEAKER_01]: The point is, and then again, and then again.

136
00:11:22,215 --> 00:11:32,840
[SPEAKER_01]: And then again, so you already kind of have a very good inkling that, oh, so there is no end to this, and I'm kind of enslaved by it, and that's not a good position to be in.

137
00:11:33,861 --> 00:11:38,743
[SPEAKER_01]: But, hey, I'll forget about that, because I'll chase the next desire, and next desire, and next desire.

138
00:11:39,384 --> 00:11:40,784
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how people go through life.

139
00:11:42,885 --> 00:11:44,206
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, you can do that.

140
00:11:44,486 --> 00:11:45,447
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody says you shouldn't.

141
00:11:46,027 --> 00:11:50,249
[SPEAKER_01]: But, again, there are the consequences, and you will have to pay the price for it.

142
00:11:52,243 --> 00:11:57,227
[SPEAKER_01]: Price of eating, consuming, and taking all these things up that don't belong to you.

143
00:11:57,347 --> 00:12:01,891
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, when in the end, the bill comes, and you realize, oh, now I have to pay for all of that.

144
00:12:02,091 --> 00:12:02,952
[SPEAKER_01]: I thought it was mine.

145
00:12:03,052 --> 00:12:04,013
[SPEAKER_01]: I thought I was the owner.

146
00:12:04,033 --> 00:12:04,753
[SPEAKER_01]: I thought it was free.

147
00:12:06,475 --> 00:12:07,315
[SPEAKER_01]: But, it's not.

148
00:12:08,436 --> 00:12:10,438
[SPEAKER_01]: If it were free, it wouldn't be pressuring you so much.

149
00:12:11,339 --> 00:12:12,860
[SPEAKER_01]: It wouldn't be enticing you so much.

150
00:12:12,940 --> 00:12:14,221
[SPEAKER_01]: It wouldn't be trapping you so much.

151
00:12:14,261 --> 00:12:15,562
[SPEAKER_01]: It wouldn't be baiting you so much.

152
00:12:29,152 --> 00:12:36,101
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when lust for the five cores of sensual pleasure is fully understood, there is no fetter bound by which the noble disciple might come back to this world.

153
00:12:36,141 --> 00:12:36,321
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

154
00:12:36,502 --> 00:12:38,264
[SPEAKER_01]: So, fully understanding that lust and

155
00:12:43,068 --> 00:12:50,615
[SPEAKER_01]: Assumptions in regard to the solid food, you will fully understand and remove the ownership of the physical body that depends on the solid food.

156
00:12:51,475 --> 00:12:52,957
[SPEAKER_01]: It won't be taken as yours anymore.

157
00:12:53,477 --> 00:13:04,467
[SPEAKER_01]: By fully understanding the body, as Buddha said in another sutta, by fully understanding, by being fully developed in regard to your own body, you will fully overcome the lust towards the five codes of sensual pleasures.

158
00:13:06,468 --> 00:13:08,330
[SPEAKER_01]: So, therefore, by fully understanding the solid food,

159
00:13:26,309 --> 00:13:32,214
[SPEAKER_00]: Suppose there is a flayed cow, a cow with no skin on it.

160
00:13:33,315 --> 00:13:38,880
[SPEAKER_00]: If she stands exposed to a wall, the creatures dwelling in the wall would nibble at her.

161
00:13:39,681 --> 00:13:43,564
[SPEAKER_00]: If she stands exposed to a tree, the creatures dwelling in the tree would bite at her.

162
00:13:44,445 --> 00:13:45,286
[SPEAKER_00]: If she stands exposed

163
00:13:55,704 --> 00:14:01,609
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever that flake cow stands exposed to, the creatures dwelling there would nibble at her.

164
00:14:03,190 --> 00:14:06,452
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's why I like to translate phassa, contact, as pressure.

165
00:14:09,014 --> 00:14:14,539
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a impersonal, mechanical or biological contact.

166
00:14:15,419 --> 00:14:18,982
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, it's the mental pressure.

167
00:14:21,104 --> 00:14:24,146
[SPEAKER_01]: Phassa is the pressure to act on account of what you felt.

168
00:14:25,998 --> 00:14:28,280
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly how a cow... how the cow is feeling.

169
00:14:29,361 --> 00:14:39,449
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not just, oh, yes, this is just a... And so, whatever insects are just anatomically chewing on my flesh, like, as a matter of fact.

170
00:14:40,030 --> 00:14:45,094
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's pressured, it's harassed, it's annoyed, it's... it's attacked, it's devoured.

171
00:14:47,436 --> 00:14:48,257
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's uncomfortable.

172
00:14:49,037 --> 00:14:49,998
[SPEAKER_01]: So, contact is uncomfortable.

173
00:14:56,457 --> 00:14:59,618
[SPEAKER_01]: Or do you not even see it as a pressure at all?

174
00:15:03,079 --> 00:15:08,341
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in that case, your understanding or your idea of passa is very inaccurate.

175
00:15:09,762 --> 00:15:11,182
[SPEAKER_01]: And usually, it is very inaccurate.

176
00:15:11,202 --> 00:15:23,607
[SPEAKER_01]: People usually think, due to the, you know, the whole commentarial misrepresentation, like, it's like this, it's just a, like a physical coming of the senses and then, poof, the result of this is the sight and the sound.

177
00:15:24,717 --> 00:15:27,618
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you know, nothing to do with me and I'm not responsible for it.

178
00:15:28,198 --> 00:15:28,578
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, no.

179
00:15:29,198 --> 00:15:36,099
[SPEAKER_01]: Passa is basically, yes, having eyes and sights, but being pressured on account of what's felt in regard to that.

180
00:15:36,539 --> 00:15:37,659
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what passa is.

181
00:15:39,039 --> 00:15:50,902
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, if people want to understand passa, want to understand the situation of that cow, find things within your mind where you are emotionally pressured, on account of anything.

182
00:15:51,362 --> 00:15:52,942
[SPEAKER_01]: Sight, sounds, mouth, taste, touch, thought.

183
00:15:53,242 --> 00:15:53,722
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't matter.

184
00:15:54,578 --> 00:15:58,479
[SPEAKER_01]: If there is a pressure there, if there is a discomfort there, that's where passa is.

185
00:15:59,359 --> 00:16:00,760
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what needs to be understood.

186
00:16:01,180 --> 00:16:08,742
[SPEAKER_01]: Not psychologically or anatomically explained, in terms of sense organs and what, like, whatever else, biology and so on.

187
00:16:09,422 --> 00:16:10,383
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not the passa.

188
00:16:11,703 --> 00:16:12,683
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it makes no difference.

189
00:16:12,703 --> 00:16:18,685
[SPEAKER_01]: So, imagine the cow being devoured by the insects and you're telling it, oh, no, see, this is what's happening to you.

190
00:16:18,745 --> 00:16:20,786
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, yeah, but I'm still pressured, I still suffer.

191
00:16:21,266 --> 00:16:22,246
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that knowledge of

192
00:16:22,951 --> 00:16:26,372
[SPEAKER_01]: explanation of the contact is not freeing me from this discomfort.

193
00:16:26,452 --> 00:16:35,594
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, it's not the knowledge, because if the knowledge truly pertains to the nature of that phenomena, it would be resulting in freedom from suffering, as the suttas would say.

194
00:16:35,614 --> 00:16:44,235
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this full understanding of contact results in you being free from the pressure.

195
00:16:44,336 --> 00:16:49,797
[SPEAKER_00]: It is in such a way, because, as I say, nutriment of contact should be seen.

196
00:16:50,057 --> 00:16:50,237
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

197
00:16:50,517 --> 00:16:51,417
[SPEAKER_00]: When the nutriment of contact

198
00:16:54,218 --> 00:16:54,598
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

199
00:16:55,499 --> 00:16:57,221
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's where the pressure is.

200
00:16:57,281 --> 00:17:00,944
[SPEAKER_01]: It's where the feeling is.

201
00:17:00,964 --> 00:17:03,907
[SPEAKER_01]: So, fully understand the nature of pressure.

202
00:17:04,808 --> 00:17:11,975
[SPEAKER_01]: You have fully uprooted any resistance or welcoming towards feelings, irrespective feelings.

203
00:17:24,023 --> 00:17:24,844
[SPEAKER_00]: By understanding.

204
00:17:25,364 --> 00:17:30,448
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, by understanding the pressure in regard to the currently enduring feeling.

205
00:17:33,150 --> 00:17:44,898
[SPEAKER_01]: Not by explaining it, but by this understanding its nature, that will kind of, you know, result in complete freedom from it.

206
00:17:47,400 --> 00:17:52,704
[SPEAKER_01]: So you subjected... Yeah, so you could... you could look into it, but first you need to feel the pressure.

207
00:17:59,008 --> 00:18:02,991
[SPEAKER_01]: You first need to feel the pressure on account of that whatever feeling is present there.

208
00:18:03,191 --> 00:18:03,852
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's there.

209
00:18:05,173 --> 00:18:06,594
[SPEAKER_01]: Some pressure always has to be there.

210
00:18:06,614 --> 00:18:09,936
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't like, oh, now, in the moment, I'm in between the contacts.

211
00:18:11,377 --> 00:18:12,078
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm in limbo.

212
00:18:12,378 --> 00:18:12,518
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

213
00:18:12,718 --> 00:18:15,460
[SPEAKER_01]: There's always something felt, which means there's always something pressuring you.

214
00:18:16,141 --> 00:18:17,282
[SPEAKER_01]: Big or small, doesn't matter.

215
00:18:17,322 --> 00:18:18,022
[SPEAKER_01]: Pressure is there.

216
00:18:19,403 --> 00:18:23,867
[SPEAKER_01]: So, first, you need to stop acting out of that pressure habitually.

217
00:18:23,907 --> 00:18:25,308
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, allow the feeling to endure.

218
00:18:27,085 --> 00:18:32,833
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, the phenomenon of pressure that manifests in regard to that, then you can start interrogating the properties of it.

219
00:18:34,776 --> 00:18:36,318
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is what we said in the previous talk.

220
00:18:36,979 --> 00:18:40,924
[SPEAKER_01]: In itself, the pressure to act is enough.

221
00:18:41,605 --> 00:18:43,748
[SPEAKER_01]: In itself, it does not imply any action.

222
00:18:54,853 --> 00:18:59,217
[SPEAKER_01]: You assume the act out of pressure to relieve the pressure, because you don't want the pressure.

223
00:19:00,078 --> 00:19:05,163
[SPEAKER_01]: If you remove the gratuitous resistance to the pressure, in itself, pressure is just the pressure.

224
00:19:05,303 --> 00:19:07,245
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, it's not pressuring you anymore.

225
00:19:10,108 --> 00:19:18,416
[SPEAKER_01]: But for that, the mind needs to be developed, in order to see that without giving in to that subtle, I don't want this, or I want this.

226
00:19:24,763 --> 00:19:27,584
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't find the right second or moment in between to catch it.

227
00:19:27,624 --> 00:19:27,784
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

228
00:19:28,464 --> 00:19:30,105
[SPEAKER_01]: How do you become imperturbable?

229
00:19:30,165 --> 00:19:32,306
[SPEAKER_01]: By practicing not being perturbed.

230
00:19:32,986 --> 00:19:33,627
[SPEAKER_01]: By enduring.

231
00:19:34,427 --> 00:19:35,327
[SPEAKER_01]: You stop moving.

232
00:19:37,668 --> 00:19:41,690
[SPEAKER_01]: And the pressure to move will fade away, because you're not feeling it anymore.

233
00:19:43,631 --> 00:19:49,853
[SPEAKER_01]: But not to practice imperturbability and not moving in regard to everything and nothing.

234
00:19:50,588 --> 00:19:50,808
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

235
00:19:51,368 --> 00:19:55,830
[SPEAKER_01]: In regard to the pressure that's there on account of what you're currently feeling.

236
00:19:56,851 --> 00:20:04,394
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why you had these misguided ascetic practices, where people endured so much, yet remained equally ignorant.

237
00:20:05,214 --> 00:20:05,794
[SPEAKER_00]: Because they moved.

238
00:20:05,854 --> 00:20:06,314
[SPEAKER_00]: They moved.

239
00:20:06,575 --> 00:20:07,135
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, exactly.

240
00:20:07,355 --> 00:20:12,237
[SPEAKER_01]: They were not moving on the level that it didn't matter whether you move or not, but they were moving on the level where it mattered.

241
00:20:14,318 --> 00:20:19,760
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to find that level of presently enduring feeling and don't move there.

242
00:20:20,545 --> 00:20:21,406
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything else is fine.

243
00:20:21,746 --> 00:20:22,947
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything else is second to it.

244
00:20:23,067 --> 00:20:26,931
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything else will fall in place if you uproot the craving on the level where it matters.

245
00:20:28,893 --> 00:20:35,099
[SPEAKER_00]: So, and the third nutrient, mental intention, mental position.

246
00:20:36,134 --> 00:20:42,338
[SPEAKER_00]: Suppose there is a charcoal pit, deeper than a man's height, filled with glowing coals, with flame or smoke.

247
00:20:43,118 --> 00:20:50,142
[SPEAKER_00]: A man would come along wanting to live, not wanting to die, desiring happiness and averse to suffering.

248
00:20:50,162 --> 00:20:51,163
[SPEAKER_00]: Then two strong men,

249
00:20:51,946 --> 00:20:55,629
[SPEAKER_00]: would grab him by both arms and drag him towards the charcoal pit.

250
00:20:56,469 --> 00:21:00,952
[SPEAKER_00]: The man's intention, volition, would be to get far away.

251
00:21:01,313 --> 00:21:03,034
[SPEAKER_00]: His longing would be to get far away.

252
00:21:03,714 --> 00:21:07,117
[SPEAKER_00]: His wish would be to get far away from that, from the charcoal pit.

253
00:21:07,717 --> 00:21:08,378
[SPEAKER_00]: For what reason?

254
00:21:08,738 --> 00:21:14,782
[SPEAKER_00]: Because he knows, I will fall into this charcoal pit, and on account of that, I will meet death or deadly suffering.

255
00:21:16,543 --> 00:21:21,865
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it is in such a way that, because, that I say, the nutriment of mental intention should be seen.

256
00:21:23,386 --> 00:21:29,449
[SPEAKER_00]: When that mental intention is fully understood, the three kinds of craving are fully understood.

257
00:21:32,090 --> 00:21:34,731
[SPEAKER_00]: So, mental intention should be seen.

258
00:21:34,951 --> 00:21:35,552
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

259
00:21:35,592 --> 00:21:41,214
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what would practically, what would, what would be a practical example of mental intention?

260
00:21:42,515 --> 00:21:43,255
[SPEAKER_01]: I want to go.

261
00:21:43,395 --> 00:21:43,835
[SPEAKER_01]: Is what?

262
00:21:44,135 --> 00:21:44,516
[SPEAKER_01]: I want.

263
00:21:45,136 --> 00:21:45,516
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

264
00:21:48,913 --> 00:21:50,134
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I desire...

265
00:21:50,554 --> 00:21:52,255
[SPEAKER_01]: I want, I want this, I don't want that.

266
00:21:53,475 --> 00:22:00,819
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you can't, like, oh, okay, I don't want, I don't want my wanting, or I don't want anything, because that's not what you want.

267
00:22:04,942 --> 00:22:07,883
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't just want your way out of your desire.

268
00:22:10,605 --> 00:22:13,126
[SPEAKER_01]: But, it is the attitude of wanting.

269
00:22:13,706 --> 00:22:15,027
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, what do you want?

270
00:22:17,893 --> 00:22:25,918
[SPEAKER_01]: Are those wants of yours still pertaining to the five codes of sensual pleasure, one way or the other, subtle or gross?

271
00:22:27,078 --> 00:22:36,004
[SPEAKER_01]: Are they still pertaining to, you know, gratuitous assumption of safety in this world, gratuitous assumption of ownership, my own sense of self?

272
00:22:37,945 --> 00:22:40,166
[SPEAKER_01]: Are those wants still revolving around those things?

273
00:22:40,586 --> 00:22:41,567
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what needs to change.

274
00:22:44,028 --> 00:22:46,750
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha says, you know, you replace the joys

275
00:22:48,558 --> 00:22:54,220
[SPEAKER_01]: of sensual life with the joys of renunciate life, of a life that says no to sensuality.

276
00:22:54,961 --> 00:23:03,504
[SPEAKER_01]: You replace the miseries, the suffering of the sensual life, with the suffering of the renunciate life, because it's better.

277
00:23:04,385 --> 00:23:11,088
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not optional experiencing joys and miseries, for as long as you have the body.

278
00:23:11,988 --> 00:23:13,188
[SPEAKER_01]: But what's optional is

279
00:23:15,443 --> 00:23:25,368
[SPEAKER_01]: what joys and miseries you'll experience, whether they're going to be pure or not, whether they're going to be causing more attachment or not, whether they're going to be causing more infatuation and lust or not.

280
00:23:26,509 --> 00:23:35,293
[SPEAKER_01]: So, replace the sensual ones with the renunciate ones, and sustain that, and there will be no more problems for you.

281
00:23:36,514 --> 00:23:39,215
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can want everywhere else.

282
00:23:40,336 --> 00:23:42,457
[SPEAKER_01]: You can want anything else, except the five

283
00:23:45,620 --> 00:23:53,606
[SPEAKER_01]: Sustain that context and you will overcome lust towards centrality, because you're not feeding it anymore through gratuitous sustaining of wanting it, valuing it.

284
00:23:54,867 --> 00:23:55,908
[SPEAKER_01]: Anywhere except there.

285
00:23:56,308 --> 00:23:57,789
[SPEAKER_01]: Anywhere except there, yeah.

286
00:23:58,550 --> 00:24:03,494
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's, you know, the joys of seclusion used to replace the joys of a household life.

287
00:24:04,515 --> 00:24:11,100
[SPEAKER_01]: Joys of... pains of seclusion, pains of renunciation, replace the pains of a household life, which are far worse.

288
00:24:11,992 --> 00:24:18,594
[SPEAKER_01]: Far more burdening and far more, like, um, carrying, like, much, much further reaching consequences.

289
00:24:20,115 --> 00:24:27,498
[SPEAKER_01]: Things you do on account of sensuality pertain, then, you know, consequences come, like, in lives to come, not just, not just in this one.

290
00:24:28,458 --> 00:24:32,119
[SPEAKER_01]: But pains of seclusion, renunciation, pretty much end there.

291
00:24:33,620 --> 00:24:39,922
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there's nothing done on account of it, no other people affected by it, so it's not, does not carry the same significance.

292
00:24:40,771 --> 00:24:45,873
[SPEAKER_01]: And yet, you used it to now replace all this other burden that would have otherwise caused you so much more hassle.

293
00:24:50,334 --> 00:24:51,755
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the mental wanting.

294
00:24:52,335 --> 00:25:09,541
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like that you cannot have it, but you should certainly make sure that none of it is rooted in the domain of the senses, in the domain of, as I said, ownership and, like, is your wanting based on just pleasure?

295
00:25:11,227 --> 00:25:11,787
[SPEAKER_01]: More pleasure?

296
00:25:12,768 --> 00:25:14,308
[SPEAKER_01]: Gratuitous sense of safety?

297
00:25:14,368 --> 00:25:16,809
[SPEAKER_01]: Or is your wanting based on practicality, for example?

298
00:25:17,749 --> 00:25:19,610
[SPEAKER_01]: Practicality suitable for practice?

299
00:25:20,530 --> 00:25:21,050
[SPEAKER_01]: Enough food.

300
00:25:21,450 --> 00:25:27,492
[SPEAKER_01]: Enough food, enough shelter, enough robes or clothes, enough means to live by.

301
00:25:28,573 --> 00:25:30,733
[SPEAKER_01]: Or is your wanting based on much more than that?

302
00:25:33,714 --> 00:25:38,776
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's replacing the bad wanting with good wanting, let's say like that, in simple terms.

303
00:25:40,947 --> 00:25:46,990
[SPEAKER_01]: And then sustaining the good wanting until you can completely disown even that.

304
00:26:01,776 --> 00:26:02,897
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is it called nutrient?

305
00:26:04,257 --> 00:26:10,700
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, because your sense of self, your being, all of your assumptions are fed by it.

306
00:26:12,042 --> 00:26:13,483
[SPEAKER_01]: Depend on it, sustain by it.

307
00:26:13,903 --> 00:26:15,324
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I keep saying sustaining it.

308
00:26:15,564 --> 00:26:18,286
[SPEAKER_01]: It sustains it, whether you want it or not.

309
00:26:18,646 --> 00:26:20,087
[SPEAKER_01]: Through acting, you are feeding it.

310
00:26:22,129 --> 00:26:33,877
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's a direct nutriment for bhava, for ego, for conceit, for everything, for ownership.

311
00:26:33,897 --> 00:26:34,817
[SPEAKER_01]: Vishya circle, yeah.

312
00:26:37,419 --> 00:26:39,821
[SPEAKER_00]: So, food will maintain that bhava?

313
00:26:42,214 --> 00:26:43,315
[SPEAKER_01]: Food is eating.

314
00:26:43,335 --> 00:26:46,737
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, see, four types of nutriment.

315
00:26:47,217 --> 00:26:49,438
[SPEAKER_01]: But what's in common to all those nutriments?

316
00:26:49,639 --> 00:26:54,982
[SPEAKER_01]: They are nutriments for bhava and for your sense of self, when you take them wrongly.

317
00:26:56,263 --> 00:27:02,006
[SPEAKER_01]: So, see, the one who is free from nutriment of food, an arahant, doesn't stop eating food.

318
00:27:03,447 --> 00:27:11,692
[SPEAKER_01]: But eating food ceases to be the nutriment for his being, because he's eating it like his dead child that he just killed in order to survive.

319
00:27:13,922 --> 00:27:19,085
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no wanting anymore, no desire, no lust in regard to the nutrimental food.

320
00:27:19,306 --> 00:27:21,127
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, it has ceased to be the nutriment.

321
00:27:22,668 --> 00:27:26,230
[SPEAKER_01]: Nutriment for bhava, nutriment for his being and misconceptions.

322
00:27:27,451 --> 00:27:28,612
[SPEAKER_01]: And there's contact for him.

323
00:27:29,693 --> 00:27:30,053
[SPEAKER_01]: Passa.

324
00:27:30,073 --> 00:27:30,353
[SPEAKER_01]: Passa.

325
00:27:30,393 --> 00:27:31,274
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no pressure.

326
00:27:31,374 --> 00:27:31,734
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

327
00:27:31,794 --> 00:27:33,235
[SPEAKER_01]: Pressure, again, was your attitude.

328
00:27:33,996 --> 00:27:35,837
[SPEAKER_01]: As I said, itch is enough in itself.

329
00:27:36,906 --> 00:27:39,208
[SPEAKER_01]: Does not require a scratch, nor even imply.

330
00:27:39,608 --> 00:27:40,509
[SPEAKER_01]: But there's still an itch.

331
00:27:40,929 --> 00:27:44,652
[SPEAKER_01]: There is still an itch, but it does not pressure towards scratching.

332
00:27:45,092 --> 00:27:45,933
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just an itch.

333
00:27:47,714 --> 00:27:57,482
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you are not developed in regard to the itch, your mind cannot resist the thoughts, the delight, the pressure, the possibility of scratch.

334
00:27:58,243 --> 00:27:59,444
[SPEAKER_01]: Remove the itch.

335
00:28:00,324 --> 00:28:02,366
[SPEAKER_01]: But itching itself is self-sufficient.

336
00:28:02,566 --> 00:28:02,966
[SPEAKER_01]: It's enough.

337
00:28:03,407 --> 00:28:04,407
[SPEAKER_01]: In itch, there's just itch.

338
00:28:07,321 --> 00:28:12,983
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you start seeing it like that, you will not be pressured to scratch it.

339
00:28:14,444 --> 00:28:16,905
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the pressure for scratching it will fade away.

340
00:28:20,006 --> 00:28:25,528
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, those intentions, that wanting, won't be feeding that bhāva either.

341
00:28:25,648 --> 00:28:26,628
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, exactly, yeah.

342
00:28:27,549 --> 00:28:36,412
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why in another sutra, the Buddha said, you know, passa, contact, cannot contact the groundless one, the one that has no ground for that pressure to land,

343
00:28:38,148 --> 00:28:42,552
[SPEAKER_01]: No ownership, no delight, no lust in those thoughts of what if I only scratch it.

344
00:28:42,952 --> 00:28:44,514
[SPEAKER_01]: None of that.

345
00:28:44,534 --> 00:28:45,735
[SPEAKER_01]: He completely disowned.

346
00:28:46,275 --> 00:28:52,981
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, the thoughts of scratching do not pressure him anymore.

347
00:28:53,001 --> 00:28:57,084
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the itch is there, the thoughts of scratching might be there, but there is no more pressure to scratch.

348
00:29:01,448 --> 00:29:03,390
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, even the thoughts of scratching will fade away.

349
00:29:04,139 --> 00:29:05,840
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, again, in the itch, there's just the itch.

350
00:29:05,940 --> 00:29:07,141
[SPEAKER_01]: In the sin, there's just the sin.

351
00:29:07,181 --> 00:29:08,701
[SPEAKER_01]: In the hurt, there's just the hurt, and so on.

352
00:29:10,182 --> 00:29:11,923
[SPEAKER_00]: It's no longer being fueled, isn't it?

353
00:29:11,943 --> 00:29:13,444
[SPEAKER_01]: No longer being fueled, yeah, yeah.

354
00:29:13,644 --> 00:29:14,104
[SPEAKER_01]: Nutrients.

355
00:29:14,144 --> 00:29:14,324
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

356
00:29:15,265 --> 00:29:16,245
[SPEAKER_01]: And same with the wanting.

357
00:29:19,827 --> 00:29:22,569
[SPEAKER_00]: And the the last one, number four,

358
00:29:26,320 --> 00:29:27,621
[SPEAKER_00]: Still alive, sāyā.

359
00:29:53,070 --> 00:29:56,311
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, go at noon, strike him with a hundred spears.

360
00:29:57,512 --> 00:29:59,793
[SPEAKER_00]: At noon, they strike him with a hundred spears.

361
00:29:59,973 --> 00:30:03,594
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, in the evening, the king asks, Men, how's that man?

362
00:30:04,315 --> 00:30:05,495
[SPEAKER_00]: Still alive, sire.

363
00:30:06,155 --> 00:30:09,577
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, go and, in the evening, strike him with a hundred spears.

364
00:30:09,977 --> 00:30:11,898
[SPEAKER_00]: In the evening, they strike him with a hundred spears.

365
00:30:13,238 --> 00:30:13,919
[SPEAKER_00]: What do you think?

366
00:30:14,039 --> 00:30:17,040
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, would that man be struck with three hundred spears?

367
00:30:24,795 --> 00:30:32,680
[SPEAKER_00]: Venerable Sir, even if he were struck with one spear, he would experience pain and displeasure on that account, not to speak of 300 spears.

368
00:30:34,021 --> 00:30:39,204
[SPEAKER_00]: It is in such a way, because that I say the nutriment consciousness should be seen.

369
00:30:39,924 --> 00:30:43,406
[SPEAKER_00]: When that is fully understood, name and form is fully understood.

370
00:30:44,066 --> 00:30:45,507
[SPEAKER_01]: The all has been fully understood.

371
00:30:45,527 --> 00:30:49,910
[SPEAKER_01]: So you're saying... Yeah, so...

372
00:31:02,390 --> 00:31:11,996
[SPEAKER_00]: Presence, life, the... Why do all those things have in common?

373
00:31:12,096 --> 00:31:14,718
[SPEAKER_01]: Even the etymology of consciousness, cognizing.

374
00:31:15,539 --> 00:31:16,479
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they are cognized.

375
00:31:16,679 --> 00:31:17,060
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

376
00:31:18,541 --> 00:31:20,182
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's the subtlest, obviously.

377
00:31:20,342 --> 00:31:21,503
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the subtlest nutriment.

378
00:31:22,323 --> 00:31:29,708
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, see, understanding the nutriment of food, you'll be an anāgāmī, but there will still be, you know, your, like, subtler

379
00:31:30,459 --> 00:31:36,220
[SPEAKER_01]: Forms of lust and aversion for being, for non-being, and so on, conceit would be left.

380
00:31:37,481 --> 00:31:47,503
[SPEAKER_01]: And, eh... Consciousness is pretty much, if... the subtlest form, the subtlest type of nutriment, because it's on the level of just mere cognizing of things.

381
00:31:49,224 --> 00:31:52,184
[SPEAKER_01]: But are you cognizing those things with implication for me?

382
00:31:54,245 --> 00:31:54,585
[SPEAKER_01]: Mine?

383
00:31:55,305 --> 00:31:55,645
[SPEAKER_01]: I am?

384
00:31:56,966 --> 00:31:58,586
[SPEAKER_01]: Or are you cognizing those things as

385
00:31:59,116 --> 00:32:01,316
[SPEAKER_01]: Not for me, not mine, not I am.

386
00:32:02,037 --> 00:32:08,058
[SPEAKER_01]: As the Buddha encouraged the the noble disciples to to regard everything not for me, not mine, not I am.

387
00:32:12,439 --> 00:32:28,302
[SPEAKER_01]: So, anything you cognize, is it cognized with the implication for me, I am, in regard to this, as Mūla Parīhā Sutta says, conceive in, apart from,

388
00:32:28,988 --> 00:32:29,308
[SPEAKER_01]: Mine.

389
00:32:33,191 --> 00:32:36,133
[SPEAKER_01]: Or are you cognizing it without it, without all that?

390
00:32:39,295 --> 00:32:40,676
[SPEAKER_00]: Why the simile of spears?

391
00:32:40,696 --> 00:32:41,117
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is it that?

392
00:32:41,737 --> 00:32:48,722
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, because it's so subtle that you can't, you know, you can't cognize your cognizing correctly.

393
00:32:49,583 --> 00:32:54,246
[SPEAKER_01]: Because any form of cognizing already carries that conceit for me and mine.

394
00:32:54,987 --> 00:32:56,828
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only way to penetrate through that

395
00:32:57,428 --> 00:33:14,132
[SPEAKER_01]: Is to, again, same like the simile of eating your own child, to regard it as the most perilous thing in the world, that's... that must not be wanted, and then sustain in that context, as the framework for any cognizing that arises, until that cognizing is purified.

396
00:33:14,373 --> 00:33:16,573
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't do it on the level of cognizing.

397
00:33:16,653 --> 00:33:17,433
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just too subtle.

398
00:33:19,314 --> 00:33:21,814
[SPEAKER_01]: And your tool for doing it is cognizing.

399
00:33:21,975 --> 00:33:23,655
[SPEAKER_01]: So your tools are corrupted already.

400
00:33:27,585 --> 00:33:30,947
[SPEAKER_01]: But what you can do is develop the framework of...

401
00:33:30,967 --> 00:33:38,693
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so, any form of cognition, any situation I become self-aware in, is like being struck with a spear.

402
00:33:39,633 --> 00:33:43,956
[SPEAKER_01]: Paddle, pain, possibility of death.

403
00:33:43,976 --> 00:33:44,977
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what next?

404
00:33:45,057 --> 00:33:45,377
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing.

405
00:33:45,737 --> 00:33:51,661
[SPEAKER_01]: Sustain that attitude, there will be no more basis for passion and lust to endure, and to proliferate.

406
00:33:53,042 --> 00:33:56,925
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they will have to... like, that's heating up the pan, as the Buddha gave that simile.

407
00:33:58,674 --> 00:33:59,955
[SPEAKER_01]: The pan is really hot.

408
00:34:00,516 --> 00:34:03,498
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you throw a lot of water on it, it will evaporate instantly.

409
00:34:04,959 --> 00:34:12,966
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's how you get rid of the conceit that's affecting your cognizing, by providing a hot pan for it.

410
00:34:13,966 --> 00:34:14,867
[SPEAKER_01]: That context.

411
00:34:15,408 --> 00:34:15,748
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

412
00:34:15,808 --> 00:34:17,729
[SPEAKER_01]: Context is the hot pan.

413
00:34:20,512 --> 00:34:21,753
[SPEAKER_00]: The context of not-mind.

414
00:34:21,773 --> 00:34:24,315
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whatever you experience... Well, not just not-mind.

415
00:34:24,475 --> 00:34:25,516
[SPEAKER_01]: That's, again, too abstract.

416
00:34:25,536 --> 00:34:28,278
[SPEAKER_01]: The context of this is my death.

417
00:34:29,958 --> 00:34:33,061
[SPEAKER_01]: This is a spear that I might die as a result of.

418
00:34:33,702 --> 00:34:35,844
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, that's the contemplation of death.

419
00:34:36,965 --> 00:34:49,517
[SPEAKER_01]: If your context of death is in regard to even in-breath or out-breath, there, unchanged, sustaining the context, yeah, all the lust, passion, conceit will have to evaporate from your experience.

420
00:34:49,557 --> 00:34:52,620
[SPEAKER_01]: Cannot stay there, because the context is just frying it all up.

421
00:35:07,191 --> 00:35:18,618
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whatever is presence, present, whatever I experience, a sight, sound... Well, it's not even... you don't want to start on that particular level of sight, spear, spear, spear.

422
00:35:18,638 --> 00:35:18,938
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

423
00:35:19,138 --> 00:35:19,738
[SPEAKER_01]: Cognizing.

424
00:35:19,818 --> 00:35:22,000
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is your current situation?

425
00:35:22,080 --> 00:35:23,520
[SPEAKER_01]: What is your current state of mind?

426
00:35:23,941 --> 00:35:25,562
[SPEAKER_01]: What is your current enduring feeling?

427
00:35:25,962 --> 00:35:27,443
[SPEAKER_01]: What's your current body posture?

428
00:35:27,563 --> 00:35:29,784
[SPEAKER_01]: All this level, all this form of cognizing.

429
00:35:31,505 --> 00:35:36,568
[SPEAKER_01]: So, on that general level, on that peripheral level, not I'm cognizing the table, I'm cognizing the book,

430
00:35:37,003 --> 00:35:38,164
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm cognizing the phone.

431
00:35:38,605 --> 00:35:38,805
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

432
00:35:39,666 --> 00:35:43,629
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm cognizing my current situation of such-and-such feeling, enduring, and I'm sitting here.

433
00:35:44,310 --> 00:35:45,171
[SPEAKER_01]: That's despair.

434
00:35:45,691 --> 00:35:49,094
[SPEAKER_01]: My... the broadest context I can find is despair.

435
00:35:49,295 --> 00:35:51,276
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how you broaden even that context.

436
00:35:52,498 --> 00:36:02,607
[SPEAKER_01]: Broaden in a sense of... develop it further, so it becomes even more unsuitable for passion, for carelessness, for ownership.

437
00:36:05,238 --> 00:36:09,740
[SPEAKER_01]: And if it's unpleasant, it's only unpleasant because of your ownership that's there.

438
00:36:10,761 --> 00:36:13,122
[SPEAKER_01]: If there is no ownership, it would not be unpleasant.

439
00:36:13,842 --> 00:36:17,704
[SPEAKER_01]: Same like when you put, you know, disinfecting alcohol on your skin.

440
00:36:18,045 --> 00:36:21,807
[SPEAKER_01]: Unless your skin is broken and infected, it won't hurt.

441
00:36:24,388 --> 00:36:26,469
[SPEAKER_01]: So it hurts only because there is a wound.

442
00:36:28,390 --> 00:36:34,473
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you're really unwise, you would think, oh no, it hurts because of alcohol, thus I won't put the disinfectant on.

443
00:36:37,598 --> 00:36:49,640
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you develop the context and it hurts, it hurts because there is a wound, which means if the wound heals, it will stop hurting without you needing to abandon the context.

444
00:36:55,181 --> 00:37:01,682
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, it... so, it says, yeah, when consciousness is fully understood, name and form is fully understood.

445
00:37:01,882 --> 00:37:04,003
[SPEAKER_01]: Anything that can manifest, however subtle.

446
00:37:04,583 --> 00:37:06,723
[SPEAKER_01]: Name and form, in other sutras, would be called the all.

447
00:37:08,593 --> 00:37:16,635
[SPEAKER_01]: Anything that can be described or designated, thought of, remembered, planned, doesn't matter, past, future, present, has to be within name and form.

448
00:37:17,135 --> 00:37:28,718
[SPEAKER_01]: So, means, if you fully developed the context in regard to this passion, towards cognizing of any kind, anything that can be cognized, will be free from passion.

449
00:37:28,898 --> 00:37:30,698
[SPEAKER_01]: You'll be fully understood.

450
00:37:30,718 --> 00:37:31,298
[SPEAKER_01]: Name and form.

451
00:37:32,139 --> 00:37:33,619
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is anything that can be cognized.

452
00:37:36,380 --> 00:37:37,700
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there's really nothing left to do then.

